(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("git",{"git/first-repository-and-commits":{"html":"\u003cp>You have a folder of scripts and a file called deploy_final_v2_REAL.sh next to deploy_final_v3.sh. Nobody knows which one runs in production or what changed between them. Version control replaces that folder-copy habit with a complete, searchable history of every change, who made it and why. Git is the tool almost every team uses for it, and its core idea is small enough to learn in one sitting.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What Git stores: snapshots, commits and a history you can search\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The cycle: edit, stage with git add, save with git commit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Read the history with git log, git diff and git show\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep build output and secrets out of the repository with .gitignore\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"snapshots-not-copies-of-files\">Snapshots, not copies of files\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Git is a \u003cstrong>distributed\u003c/strong> version control system. Every copy of a repository (a \u003cstrong>clone\u003c/strong>) holds the full history, so you can commit, compare and browse the past without a network. Each \u003cstrong>commit\u003c/strong> is a snapshot of the whole project at one moment, plus a message, an author, a timestamp and a pointer to its parent commit. Commits are identified by a long hash such as \u003ccode>3f9c2ab...\u003c/code>, and the first seven characters are usually enough to name one. Because every commit points to its parent, the history is a chain you can walk backwards.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Git stores content efficiently by keeping identical files once, so snapshots are cheap. Think of the history as a list of save points you can return to or compare at any time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"create-a-repository-and-commit\">Create a repository and commit\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Tell Git who you are once, then start a repository:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git config --global user.name &quot;Asha Rao&quot;\ngit config --global user.email &quot;asha@example.com&quot;\ngit config --global init.defaultBranch main\n\nmkdir backup-tools &amp;&amp; cd backup-tools\ngit init\necho &quot;#!/bin/sh&quot; &gt; backup.sh\ngit status\ngit add backup.sh\ngit commit -m &quot;Add backup script skeleton&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">git config --global user.name \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Asha Rao&quot;\u003c/span>\ngit config --global user.email \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;asha@example.com&quot;\u003c/span>\ngit config --global init.defaultBranch main\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">mkdir\u003c/span> backup-tools &amp;&amp; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cd\u003c/span> backup-tools\ngit init\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;#!/bin/sh&quot;\u003c/span> &gt; backup.sh\ngit status\ngit add backup.sh\ngit commit -m \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Add backup script skeleton&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>git init\u003c/code> creates a hidden \u003ccode>.git\u003c/code> directory that holds the whole history; delete it and the project is just files again. \u003ccode>git status\u003c/code> is the command you will run most, because it tells you what is changed, what is staged and what is untracked. The commit includes only what you staged with \u003ccode>git add\u003c/code>, not everything in the folder.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-three-areas\">The three areas\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Files move through three places. The \u003cstrong>working tree\u003c/strong> is the files you edit. The \u003cstrong>staging area\u003c/strong> (also called the index) is what will go into the next commit. The \u003cstrong>repository\u003c/strong> is the saved history. \u003ccode>git add\u003c/code> copies a change from the working tree into the staging area, and \u003ccode>git commit\u003c/code> turns the staged set into a permanent snapshot. This two-step design lets you edit ten files and commit them as two clean, separate changes: stage the first group, commit, then stage the rest.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>git add -p\u003c/code> lets you stage individual pieces of a file, which is how experienced people keep commits focused.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"read-the-history\">Read the history\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git log --oneline --graph --decorate -10\ngit show 3f9c2ab\ngit diff                 # working tree vs staging\ngit diff --staged        # staging vs last commit\ngit log -p -- backup.sh  # every change to one file\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">git \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> --oneline --graph --decorate -10\ngit show 3f9c2ab\ngit diff                 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># working tree vs staging\u003c/span>\ngit diff --staged        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># staging vs last commit\u003c/span>\ngit \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> -p -- backup.sh  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># every change to one file\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A good commit does one thing and its message says why. Write a short summary line in the imperative (&quot;Fix retry loop in backup.sh&quot;), leave a blank line, then explain the reason if it is not obvious. Months later, \u003ccode>git log\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>git blame\u003c/code> are your documentation of why the code looks the way it does, so a message like &quot;update&quot; wastes that chance.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"leave-things-out-with-gitignore\">Leave things out with .gitignore\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Some files do not belong in history: compiled output, virtual environments, logs, editor settings and above all secrets. List patterns in a \u003ccode>.gitignore\u003c/code> file at the top of the repository:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"__pycache__/\n*.log\n.venv/\ndist/\n.env\nterraform.tfstate*\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">__pycache__/\n*.log\n.venv/\ndist/\n.env\nterraform.tfstate*\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Ignore rules only affect files Git is not yet tracking. If a file was committed before you ignored it, it stays tracked until you remove it from the index with \u003ccode>git rm --cached FILE\u003c/code>. Add the \u003ccode>.gitignore\u003c/code> in your very first commit, because it is much easier to avoid a bad file than to remove it from history later.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You edit backup.sh and run `git commit -m &quot;fix&quot;` without `git add`. What does Git say?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> It reports &quot;no changes added to commit&quot; and makes no commit, because nothing is staged.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Commits contain only staged changes; `git commit -a` stages tracked files for you but is a shortcut, not a habit to rely on blindly.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-two-gigabytes-of-build-output\">Incident: Two gigabytes of build output\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new teammate cloned the deployment repository and waited twenty minutes for a download that should have been seconds.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git clone ssh://code.example.com/ops/deploy.git\ngit count-objects -vH | grep size-pack\ngit log --oneline --stat -3 | head\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">clone\u003c/span> ssh://code.example.com/ops/deploy.git\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Receiving objects: 100% (48211/48211), 1.92 GiB | 2.1 MiB/s\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git count-objects -vH | grep size-pack\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">size-pack: 1.88 GiB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> --oneline --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">stat\u003c/span> -3 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">9ad41e0 Add release bundles\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> release/app-1.4.tar.gz | Bin 0 -&gt; 412308120 bytes\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Someone had committed compiled release bundles several times, and because Git keeps every version forever, each bundle stayed in history even after being deleted in later commits. Removing them from history required rewriting the repository (covered in the lesson on secrets and history). The lasting fix was a .gitignore for the dist directory and a rule that release artifacts go to an artifact store, not to source control.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Committing generated files, logs or secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing messages like &quot;update&quot; or &quot;fix stuff&quot;\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Making one huge commit that mixes unrelated changes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping git status before committing\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"snapshots-not-copies-of-files","text":"Snapshots, not copies of files"},{"id":"create-a-repository-and-commit","text":"Create a repository and commit"},{"id":"the-three-areas","text":"The three areas"},{"id":"read-the-history","text":"Read the history"},{"id":"leave-things-out-with-gitignore","text":"Leave things out with .gitignore"},{"id":"incident-two-gigabytes-of-build-output","text":"Incident: Two gigabytes of build output"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Pro Git: Getting a Git Repository","u":"https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Basics-Getting-a-Git-Repository"},{"t":"Pro Git: Recording Changes to the Repository","u":"https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Basics-Recording-Changes-to-the-Repository"}],"text":"You have a folder of scripts and a file called deploy_final_v2_REAL.sh next to deploy_final_v3.sh. Nobody knows which one runs in production or what changed between them. Version control replaces that folder-copy habit with a complete, searchable history of every change, who made it and why. Git is the tool almost every team uses for it, and its core idea is small enough to learn in one sitting. What you will learn What Git stores: snapshots, commits and a history you can search The cycle: edit, stage with git add, save with git commit Read the history with git log, git diff and git show Keep build output and secrets out of the repository with .gitignore Snapshots, not copies of files Git is a distributed version control system. Every copy of a repository (a clone ) holds the full history, so you can commit, compare and browse the past without a network. Each commit is a snapshot of the whole project at one moment, plus a message, an author, a timestamp and a pointer to its parent commit. Commits are identified by a long hash such as 3f9c2ab... , and the first seven characters are usually enough to name one. Because every commit points to its parent, the history is a chain you can walk backwards. Git stores content efficiently by keeping identical files once, so snapshots are cheap. Think of the history as a list of save points you can return to or compare at any time. Create a repository and commit Tell Git who you are once, then start a repository: git config --global user.name \"Asha Rao\" git config --global user.email \"asha@example.com\" git config --global init.defaultBranch main mkdir backup-tools && cd backup-tools git init echo \"#!/bin/sh\" > backup.sh git status git add backup.sh git commit -m \"Add backup script skeleton\" git init creates a hidden .git directory that holds the whole history; delete it and the project is just files again. git status is the command you will run most, because it tells you what is changed, what is staged and what is untracked. The commit includes only what you staged with git add , not everything in the folder. The three areas Files move through three places. The working tree is the files you edit. The staging area (also called the index) is what will go into the next commit. The repository is the saved history. git add copies a change from the working tree into the staging area, and git commit turns the staged set into a permanent snapshot. This two-step design lets you edit ten files and commit them as two clean, separate changes: stage the first group, commit, then stage the rest. git add -p lets you stage individual pieces of a file, which is how experienced people keep commits focused. Read the history git log --oneline --graph --decorate -10 git show 3f9c2ab git diff # working tree vs staging git diff --staged # staging vs last commit git log -p -- backup.sh # every change to one file A good commit does one thing and its message says why. Write a short summary line in the imperative (\"Fix retry loop in backup.sh\"), leave a blank line, then explain the reason if it is not obvious. Months later, git log and git blame are your documentation of why the code looks the way it does, so a message like \"update\" wastes that chance. Leave things out with .gitignore Some files do not belong in history: compiled output, virtual environments, logs, editor settings and above all secrets. List patterns in a .gitignore file at the top of the repository: __pycache__/ *.log .venv/ dist/ .env terraform.tfstate* Ignore rules only affect files Git is not yet tracking. If a file was committed before you ignored it, it stays tracked until you remove it from the index with git rm --cached FILE . Add the .gitignore in your very first commit, because it is much easier to avoid a bad file than to remove it from history later. Predict first. You edit backup.sh and run `git commit -m \"fix\"` without `git add`. What does Git say? Your guess Answer: It reports \"no changes added to commit\" and makes no commit, because "},"git/staging-undoing-and-stashing":{"html":"\u003cp>Everyone makes mistakes in Git: a wrong file staged, a typo in the last commit message, a change that should not have been committed at all. The fear of making them is what keeps beginners away from the tool. Git has a safe undo for almost every case, but they are different commands for different situations, and the dangerous ones are the ones that rewrite history others may already have.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Discard working tree edits and unstage files with git restore\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fix the most recent commit with git commit --amend\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Undo a commit that others already have with git revert\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Understand soft, mixed and hard reset, and park work with git stash\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"undo-before-you-commit\">Undo before you commit\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Two modern commands cover the common cases:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git restore backup.sh              # throw away edits in the working tree (cannot be undone)\ngit restore --staged backup.sh     # unstage, but keep the edits\ngit restore --source=HEAD~2 backup.sh   # bring back the file as it was two commits ago\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">git restore backup.sh              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># throw away edits in the working tree (cannot be undone)\u003c/span>\ngit restore --staged backup.sh     \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># unstage, but keep the edits\u003c/span>\ngit restore --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">source\u003c/span>=HEAD~2 backup.sh   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># bring back the file as it was two commits ago\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The first one is destructive: edits that were never committed are gone, because Git never saw them as a snapshot. Read \u003ccode>git status\u003c/code> first; it prints the correct command as a hint. Older tutorials use \u003ccode>git checkout -- FILE\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>git reset HEAD FILE\u003c/code> for the same jobs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"fix-the-last-commit\">Fix the last commit\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If you forgot a file or made a typo in the message and have \u003cstrong>not pushed\u003c/strong>, amend:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git add forgotten.sh\ngit commit --amend           # opens the editor to change the message too\ngit commit --amend --no-edit # keep the message, add the staged changes\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">git add forgotten.sh\ngit commit --amend           \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># opens the editor to change the message too\u003c/span>\ngit commit --amend --no-edit \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># keep the message, add the staged changes\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Amend does not edit the old commit. It creates a new commit with a new hash and moves the branch to it, so any copy that someone else already fetched no longer matches. That is why rewriting history is fine for local work and a problem for shared work.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"undo-something-that-is-already-shared\">Undo something that is already shared\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When a commit is already on a shared branch, do not rewrite history. Add a new commit that reverses it:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git revert 3f9c2ab           # creates a new commit with the opposite change\ngit revert -m 1 7d1e0aa      # to reverse a merge commit, choose the mainline parent\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">git revert 3f9c2ab           \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># creates a new commit with the opposite change\u003c/span>\ngit revert -m 1 7d1e0aa      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># to reverse a merge commit, choose the mainline parent\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The history stays honest (&quot;this was changed, then changed back&quot;) and everyone can pull the fix normally. Revert is the right tool for production rollbacks of code, and the revert commit should say why.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reset-moving-the-branch\">Reset: moving the branch\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>git reset\u003c/code> moves the current branch pointer to another commit. The mode decides what happens to your files:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Mode\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Branch moves\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Staging area\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Working tree\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--soft\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>yes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>kept\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>kept\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--mixed\u003c/code> (default)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>yes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>reset\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>kept\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--hard\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>yes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>reset\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>overwritten\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git reset --soft HEAD~1     # undo the last commit, keep everything staged\ngit reset --hard origin/main # make my branch exactly match the remote (local work is lost)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">git reset --soft HEAD~1     \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># undo the last commit, keep everything staged\u003c/span>\ngit reset --hard origin/main \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># make my branch exactly match the remote (local work is lost)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use \u003ccode>--hard\u003c/code> only when you are sure there is nothing uncommitted you want. Even after a hard reset, committed work is usually recoverable through the reflog (see the lesson on debugging Git).\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"park-work-with-stash\">Park work with stash\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>You are in the middle of a change and need to switch tasks:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git stash push -m &quot;half-done retry logic&quot;\ngit switch hotfix/disk-alert\n# ... fix, commit, switch back ...\ngit stash list\ngit stash pop        # reapply and drop the newest stash\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">git stash push -m \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;half-done retry logic&quot;\u003c/span>\ngit switch hotfix/disk-alert\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># ... fix, commit, switch back ...\u003c/span>\ngit stash list\ngit stash pop        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># reapply and drop the newest stash\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>By default stash saves tracked changes; add \u003ccode>-u\u003c/code> to include untracked files. A stash is local and easy to forget, so treat it as a short-term parking space and commit work in progress on a branch if it will sit for days.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You pushed a commit that breaks the build and teammates have pulled it. Do you use reset --hard or revert?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Revert. It adds a new commit that reverses the change, so nobody has to repair a rewritten history.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Never rewrite commits other people already have.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-hard-reset-that-erased-a-day-of-work\">Incident: The hard reset that erased a day of work\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An engineer tried to &quot;clean up&quot; before a meeting and ran a hard reset on the wrong branch.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git status -s\ngit reset --hard origin/main\ngit status -s\ngit reflog | head -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git status -s\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> M deploy/values.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> M deploy/ingress.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git reset --hard origin/main\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">HEAD is now at 41ac9d2 Merge pull request 482\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git status -s\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git reflog | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">41ac9d2 HEAD@{0}: reset: moving to origin/main\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">7be3f10 HEAD@{1}: commit: Tune ingress timeouts\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The two uncommitted files were lost for good, because Git had never saved them. The one commit was recovered from the reflog by checking out 7be3f10 on a new branch. The team added a habit of running git status and git stash before any reset, and shell aliases that print what a hard reset will discard.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using reset --hard casually\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Amending or force-pushing commits that others already pulled\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reverting a merge without choosing the mainline parent\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving work in a stash for weeks\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"undo-before-you-commit","text":"Undo before you commit"},{"id":"fix-the-last-commit","text":"Fix the last commit"},{"id":"undo-something-that-is-already-shared","text":"Undo something that is already shared"},{"id":"reset-moving-the-branch","text":"Reset: moving the branch"},{"id":"park-work-with-stash","text":"Park work with stash"},{"id":"incident-the-hard-reset-that-erased-a-day-of-work","text":"Incident: The hard reset that erased a day of work"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Pro Git: Undoing Things","u":"https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Basics-Undoing-Things"},{"t":"git-reset documentation","u":"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-reset"}],"text":"Everyone makes mistakes in Git: a wrong file staged, a typo in the last commit message, a change that should not have been committed at all. The fear of making them is what keeps beginners away from the tool. Git has a safe undo for almost every case, but they are different commands for different situations, and the dangerous ones are the ones that rewrite history others may already have. What you will learn Discard working tree edits and unstage files with git restore Fix the most recent commit with git commit --amend Undo a commit that others already have with git revert Understand soft, mixed and hard reset, and park work with git stash Undo before you commit Two modern commands cover the common cases: git restore backup.sh # throw away edits in the working tree (cannot be undone) git restore --staged backup.sh # unstage, but keep the edits git restore -- source =HEAD~2 backup.sh # bring back the file as it was two commits ago The first one is destructive: edits that were never committed are gone, because Git never saw them as a snapshot. Read git status first; it prints the correct command as a hint. Older tutorials use git checkout -- FILE and git reset HEAD FILE for the same jobs. Fix the last commit If you forgot a file or made a typo in the message and have not pushed , amend: git add forgotten.sh git commit --amend # opens the editor to change the message too git commit --amend --no-edit # keep the message, add the staged changes Amend does not edit the old commit. It creates a new commit with a new hash and moves the branch to it, so any copy that someone else already fetched no longer matches. That is why rewriting history is fine for local work and a problem for shared work. Undo something that is already shared When a commit is already on a shared branch, do not rewrite history. Add a new commit that reverses it: git revert 3f9c2ab # creates a new commit with the opposite change git revert -m 1 7d1e0aa # to reverse a merge commit, choose the mainline parent The history stays honest (\"this was changed, then changed back\") and everyone can pull the fix normally. Revert is the right tool for production rollbacks of code, and the revert commit should say why. Reset: moving the branch git reset moves the current branch pointer to another commit. The mode decides what happens to your files: Mode Branch moves Staging area Working tree --soft yes kept kept --mixed (default) yes reset kept --hard yes reset overwritten git reset --soft HEAD~1 # undo the last commit, keep everything staged git reset --hard origin/main # make my branch exactly match the remote (local work is lost) Use --hard only when you are sure there is nothing uncommitted you want. Even after a hard reset, committed work is usually recoverable through the reflog (see the lesson on debugging Git). Park work with stash You are in the middle of a change and need to switch tasks: git stash push -m \"half-done retry logic\" git switch hotfix/disk-alert # ... fix, commit, switch back ... git stash list git stash pop # reapply and drop the newest stash By default stash saves tracked changes; add -u to include untracked files. A stash is local and easy to forget, so treat it as a short-term parking space and commit work in progress on a branch if it will sit for days. Predict first. You pushed a commit that breaks the build and teammates have pulled it. Do you use reset --hard or revert? Your guess Answer: Revert. It adds a new commit that reverses the change, so nobody has to repair a rewritten history. Never rewrite commits other people already have. Incident: The hard reset that erased a day of work Incident An engineer tried to \"clean up\" before a meeting and ran a hard reset on the wrong branch. $ git status -s M deploy/values.yaml M deploy/ingress.yaml $ git reset --hard origin/main HEAD is now at 41ac9d2 Merge pull request 482 $ git status -s $ git reflog | head -3 41ac9d2 HEAD@{0}: reset: moving to origin/main 7be3f10 HEAD@{1}: commit: Tune ingress timeouts"},"git/branches-and-merging":{"html":"\u003cp>On a team, two people change the same project at the same time. Without a way to separate their work, they overwrite each other. Branches are Git&#39;s answer: cheap, instant parallel lines of history that you combine later. Because a branch is only a pointer to a commit, creating one costs nothing, and the habit of one branch per task keeps \u003ccode>main\u003c/code> stable.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What a branch really is: a movable pointer to a commit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Create and switch branches, and see where HEAD is\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Merge with fast-forward and with merge commits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Resolve a merge conflict step by step\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-branch-is-a-pointer\">A branch is a pointer\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A branch is a small file containing the hash of one commit. When you commit on a branch, the pointer moves forward to the new commit. \u003ccode>HEAD\u003c/code> is the pointer to the branch you are on. Because nothing is copied, branching is instant even in huge repositories.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git branch                      # list local branches\ngit switch -c feature/retry     # create and move to a new branch\ngit log --oneline --graph --all --decorate\ngit switch main\ngit branch -d feature/retry     # delete a merged branch\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">git branch                      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># list local branches\u003c/span>\ngit switch -c feature/retry     \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># create and move to a new branch\u003c/span>\ngit \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> --oneline --graph --all --decorate\ngit switch main\ngit branch -d feature/retry     \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># delete a merged branch\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>git switch\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>git restore\u003c/code> replace the older, overloaded \u003ccode>git checkout\u003c/code>. Branch names with a slash (\u003ccode>feature/\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>fix/\u003c/code>) group related branches and many teams add a ticket number.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"merging\">Merging\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Merging combines the work of another branch into the current one:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git switch main\ngit merge feature/retry\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">git switch main\ngit merge feature/retry\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>If \u003ccode>main\u003c/code> has not moved since you branched, Git simply moves the pointer forward: a \u003cstrong>fast-forward\u003c/strong>, with no new commit. If both branches have new commits, Git creates a \u003cstrong>merge commit\u003c/strong> with two parents that ties the lines together. Use \u003ccode>git merge --no-ff\u003c/code> to always record a merge commit, which keeps the fact that a group of commits belonged together. Before merging, \u003ccode>git diff main...feature/retry\u003c/code> shows what the branch would add.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"conflicts\">Conflicts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>conflict\u003c/strong> happens when both branches changed the same lines. Git stops and marks the files:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt; HEAD\nretries=3\n=======\nretries=5\n&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; feature/retry\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt; HEAD\nretries=3\n=======\nretries=5\n&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; feature/retry\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The part between \u003ccode>&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>=======\u003c/code> is your side, the rest is theirs. Resolve it by editing the file to the correct final content, removing all marker lines, then:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git status                 # lists &quot;both modified&quot; files\ngit add config/backup.conf\ngit commit                 # concludes the merge\n# or abandon it:\ngit merge --abort\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">git status                 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># lists &quot;both modified&quot; files\u003c/span>\ngit add config/backup.conf\ngit commit                 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># concludes the merge\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># or abandon it:\u003c/span>\ngit merge --abort\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Do not choose a side blindly. Ask the author, run the tests and read the surrounding code, because a merge can be textually clean and still wrong, for example when one branch renames a function that the other starts to use.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keeping-branches-healthy\">Keeping branches healthy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Short-lived branches conflict less. Merge \u003ccode>main\u003c/code> into your branch (or rebase, see the next lesson) often, so differences stay small. Delete branches after they are merged. Avoid long-running branches that drift for months: the longer a branch lives, the more painful the merge becomes. Use \u003ccode>git merge --no-commit --no-ff other\u003c/code> to preview a merge, and a tool such as \u003ccode>git mergetool\u003c/code> or your editor&#39;s three-way view when conflicts are large.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> main did not change since you branched. What kind of merge is `git merge feature` and does it create a merge commit?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> A fast-forward. The pointer just moves forward and no merge commit is created unless you use --no-ff.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Fast-forward is possible only when history is a straight line.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-conflict-that-was-resolved-wrongly\">Incident: The conflict that was resolved wrongly\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A deploy script merged cleanly and failed in production an hour later.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git merge release/2.3\n./deploy.sh staging\ngit log -3 --oneline -- deploy.sh\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git merge release/2.3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Auto-merging deploy.sh\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Merge made by the 'ort' strategy.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>./deploy.sh staging\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ERROR: unknown option --region-id\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> -3 --oneline -- deploy.sh\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">8c0aa21 Rename --region-id to --region\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">4be7710 Add --region-id handling for blue/green\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>One branch had renamed an option while another had added a call using the old name. Git saw changes in different lines and merged without a conflict, but the combined result was broken. The team started running the test suite on the merge result in the pipeline, not only on each branch, and added a check that runs the script with the new options in staging before merging to main.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Long-lived branches that drift\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Resolving conflicts without understanding both sides\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving conflict markers in a file and committing it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Merging without running tests on the result\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"a-branch-is-a-pointer","text":"A branch is a pointer"},{"id":"merging","text":"Merging"},{"id":"conflicts","text":"Conflicts"},{"id":"keeping-branches-healthy","text":"Keeping branches healthy"},{"id":"incident-the-conflict-that-was-resolved-wrongly","text":"Incident: The conflict that was resolved wrongly"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Pro Git: Branches in a Nutshell","u":"https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Branching-Branches-in-a-Nutshell"},{"t":"Pro Git: Basic Branching and Merging","u":"https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Branching-Basic-Branching-and-Merging"}],"text":"On a team, two people change the same project at the same time. Without a way to separate their work, they overwrite each other. Branches are Git&#39;s answer: cheap, instant parallel lines of history that you combine later. Because a branch is only a pointer to a commit, creating one costs nothing, and the habit of one branch per task keeps main stable. What you will learn What a branch really is: a movable pointer to a commit Create and switch branches, and see where HEAD is Merge with fast-forward and with merge commits Resolve a merge conflict step by step A branch is a pointer A branch is a small file containing the hash of one commit. When you commit on a branch, the pointer moves forward to the new commit. HEAD is the pointer to the branch you are on. Because nothing is copied, branching is instant even in huge repositories. git branch # list local branches git switch -c feature/retry # create and move to a new branch git log --oneline --graph --all --decorate git switch main git branch -d feature/retry # delete a merged branch git switch and git restore replace the older, overloaded git checkout . Branch names with a slash ( feature/ , fix/ ) group related branches and many teams add a ticket number. Merging Merging combines the work of another branch into the current one: git switch main git merge feature/retry If main has not moved since you branched, Git simply moves the pointer forward: a fast-forward , with no new commit. If both branches have new commits, Git creates a merge commit with two parents that ties the lines together. Use git merge --no-ff to always record a merge commit, which keeps the fact that a group of commits belonged together. Before merging, git diff main...feature/retry shows what the branch would add. Conflicts A conflict happens when both branches changed the same lines. Git stops and marks the files: \u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c HEAD retries=3 ======= retries=5 >>>>>>> feature/retry The part between \u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c and ======= is your side, the rest is theirs. Resolve it by editing the file to the correct final content, removing all marker lines, then: git status # lists \"both modified\" files git add config/backup.conf git commit # concludes the merge # or abandon it: git merge --abort Do not choose a side blindly. Ask the author, run the tests and read the surrounding code, because a merge can be textually clean and still wrong, for example when one branch renames a function that the other starts to use. Keeping branches healthy Short-lived branches conflict less. Merge main into your branch (or rebase, see the next lesson) often, so differences stay small. Delete branches after they are merged. Avoid long-running branches that drift for months: the longer a branch lives, the more painful the merge becomes. Use git merge --no-commit --no-ff other to preview a merge, and a tool such as git mergetool or your editor&#39;s three-way view when conflicts are large. Predict first. main did not change since you branched. What kind of merge is `git merge feature` and does it create a merge commit? Your guess Answer: A fast-forward. The pointer just moves forward and no merge commit is created unless you use --no-ff. Fast-forward is possible only when history is a straight line. Incident: The conflict that was resolved wrongly Incident A deploy script merged cleanly and failed in production an hour later. $ git merge release/2.3 Auto-merging deploy.sh Merge made by the 'ort' strategy. $ ./deploy.sh staging ERROR: unknown option --region-id $ git log -3 --oneline -- deploy.sh 8c0aa21 Rename --region-id to --region 4be7710 Add --region-id handling for blue/green One branch had renamed an option while another had added a call using the old name. Git saw changes in different lines and merged without a conflict, but the combined result was broken. The team started running the test suite on the merge result in the pipeline, not only on each branch, and added a check that runs the script with the new options in staging before merging "},"git/rebase-and-clean-history":{"html":"\u003cp>Merging is safe but the history of a busy project fills with merge commits and &quot;fix typo&quot; commits that make it hard to read. Rebase lets you replay your work on top of the latest main and tidy it before others review it. It is powerful and it is the command that has caused the most Git horror stories, because it rewrites history. The rules are simple and worth learning properly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What rebase does and how it differs from merge\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Update a feature branch with git rebase main\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tidy local commits with interactive rebase (squash, reword, fixup)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The golden rule: never rebase commits that others have built on\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rebase-replays-your-commits\">Rebase replays your commits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Suppose \u003ccode>main\u003c/code> moved on while you worked on \u003ccode>feature\u003c/code>. A merge ties the two lines with a merge commit. A \u003cstrong>rebase\u003c/strong> instead takes your commits, sets them aside, moves your branch to the tip of \u003ccode>main\u003c/code>, and replays your commits one by one on top. The result looks as if you had started your work from the latest \u003ccode>main\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git switch feature/retry\ngit fetch origin\ngit rebase origin/main\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">git switch feature/retry\ngit fetch origin\ngit rebase origin/main\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The replayed commits are \u003cstrong>new commits\u003c/strong> with new hashes, though they have the same changes and messages. If a replayed commit conflicts, Git stops; resolve the files, \u003ccode>git add\u003c/code> them, and run \u003ccode>git rebase --continue\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>git rebase --abort\u003c/code> returns you to where you started.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"interactive-rebase\">Interactive rebase\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Before asking for a review, clean up your own work:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git rebase -i HEAD~4\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">git rebase -i HEAD~4\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Git opens a list of the last four commits:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"pick 1a2b3c4 Add retry loop\npick 5d6e7f8 Fix typo\npick 9a8b7c6 Add tests for retry\npick 0f1e2d3 Fix test name\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">pick 1a2b3c4 Add retry loop\npick 5d6e7f8 Fix typo\npick 9a8b7c6 Add tests for retry\npick 0f1e2d3 Fix test name\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Change the word at the start of each line: \u003ccode>reword\u003c/code> to edit a message, \u003ccode>squash\u003c/code> to merge into the previous commit and combine messages, \u003ccode>fixup\u003c/code> to merge and discard the message, \u003ccode>drop\u003c/code> to remove a commit, or reorder lines to reorder commits. Save and close, and Git rewrites the history. A small number of meaningful commits (&quot;Add retry loop&quot;, &quot;Add tests&quot;) is far easier to review than ten snapshots of your day.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The shortcut \u003ccode>git commit --fixup HASH\u003c/code> followed by \u003ccode>git rebase -i --autosquash\u003c/code> marks and moves fixes automatically.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-golden-rule\">The golden rule\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Never rebase commits that exist in a shared branch others have pulled. Rebase creates new commits, so a teammate&#39;s copy of the old ones no longer matches, and their next pull produces duplicates and confusing conflicts. It is safe to rebase your own, unpushed work or a branch only you use. If you rebased a branch you had already pushed, the push is rejected, and you need \u003ccode>git push --force-with-lease\u003c/code>, which refuses to overwrite commits you have not seen. Use it only on your own feature branches, never on \u003ccode>main\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"merge-or-rebase\">Merge or rebase?\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Both are valid, and teams choose a policy. Common choices: rebase your feature branch on \u003ccode>main\u003c/code> to keep it current, then merge with a pull request using \u003cstrong>squash\u003c/strong> (one commit per feature) or \u003cstrong>merge commit\u003c/strong> (keeps the branch history). Linear history makes \u003ccode>git bisect\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>git log\u003c/code> easier to read; merge commits preserve exactly what happened. Pick one policy per repository, write it in the contributing guide, and let the platform enforce it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You rebased a feature branch that a colleague also pulled, then force-pushed. What do they see on their next pull?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Duplicate commits or conflicts, because the history they have no longer matches the rewritten one.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Rebase makes new commits. Only rebase work nobody else has based changes on.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-force-push-that-removed-a-colleague-s-commit\">Incident: The force push that removed a colleague&#39;s commit\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Two engineers worked on the same branch and one rebased and force-pushed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git push --force origin feature/alerts\ngit log --oneline origin/feature/alerts -3\n# Priya's commit &quot;Add runbook link&quot; is no longer on the branch\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git push --force origin feature/alerts\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+ 4e2fa1b...d19c07a feature/alerts -&gt; feature/alerts (forced update)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> --oneline origin/feature/alerts -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">d19c07a Tune alert thresholds\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># Priya&#x27;s commit &quot;Add runbook link&quot; is no longer on the branch\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The force push replaced the branch and dropped a commit that the colleague had pushed a few minutes earlier. The commit was recovered from the colleague&#39;s local clone and the reflog on the server. The team switched to git push --force-with-lease, which would have refused, and agreed that shared branches are never rebased.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Rebasing a branch that others use\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using git push --force instead of --force-with-lease\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Squashing everything into a single huge commit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rebasing in the middle of a conflict without understanding the state\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"rebase-replays-your-commits","text":"Rebase replays your commits"},{"id":"interactive-rebase","text":"Interactive rebase"},{"id":"the-golden-rule","text":"The golden rule"},{"id":"merge-or-rebase","text":"Merge or rebase?"},{"id":"incident-the-force-push-that-removed-a-colleague-s-commit","text":"Incident: The force push that removed a colleague's commit"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Pro Git: Rebasing","u":"https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Branching-Rebasing"},{"t":"git-rebase documentation","u":"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-rebase"}],"text":"Merging is safe but the history of a busy project fills with merge commits and \"fix typo\" commits that make it hard to read. Rebase lets you replay your work on top of the latest main and tidy it before others review it. It is powerful and it is the command that has caused the most Git horror stories, because it rewrites history. The rules are simple and worth learning properly. What you will learn What rebase does and how it differs from merge Update a feature branch with git rebase main Tidy local commits with interactive rebase (squash, reword, fixup) The golden rule: never rebase commits that others have built on Rebase replays your commits Suppose main moved on while you worked on feature . A merge ties the two lines with a merge commit. A rebase instead takes your commits, sets them aside, moves your branch to the tip of main , and replays your commits one by one on top. The result looks as if you had started your work from the latest main . git switch feature/retry git fetch origin git rebase origin/main The replayed commits are new commits with new hashes, though they have the same changes and messages. If a replayed commit conflicts, Git stops; resolve the files, git add them, and run git rebase --continue . git rebase --abort returns you to where you started. Interactive rebase Before asking for a review, clean up your own work: git rebase -i HEAD~4 Git opens a list of the last four commits: pick 1a2b3c4 Add retry loop pick 5d6e7f8 Fix typo pick 9a8b7c6 Add tests for retry pick 0f1e2d3 Fix test name Change the word at the start of each line: reword to edit a message, squash to merge into the previous commit and combine messages, fixup to merge and discard the message, drop to remove a commit, or reorder lines to reorder commits. Save and close, and Git rewrites the history. A small number of meaningful commits (\"Add retry loop\", \"Add tests\") is far easier to review than ten snapshots of your day. The shortcut git commit --fixup HASH followed by git rebase -i --autosquash marks and moves fixes automatically. The golden rule Never rebase commits that exist in a shared branch others have pulled. Rebase creates new commits, so a teammate&#39;s copy of the old ones no longer matches, and their next pull produces duplicates and confusing conflicts. It is safe to rebase your own, unpushed work or a branch only you use. If you rebased a branch you had already pushed, the push is rejected, and you need git push --force-with-lease , which refuses to overwrite commits you have not seen. Use it only on your own feature branches, never on main . Merge or rebase? Both are valid, and teams choose a policy. Common choices: rebase your feature branch on main to keep it current, then merge with a pull request using squash (one commit per feature) or merge commit (keeps the branch history). Linear history makes git bisect and git log easier to read; merge commits preserve exactly what happened. Pick one policy per repository, write it in the contributing guide, and let the platform enforce it. Predict first. You rebased a feature branch that a colleague also pulled, then force-pushed. What do they see on their next pull? Your guess Answer: Duplicate commits or conflicts, because the history they have no longer matches the rewritten one. Rebase makes new commits. Only rebase work nobody else has based changes on. Incident: The force push that removed a colleague&#39;s commit Incident Two engineers worked on the same branch and one rebased and force-pushed. $ git push --force origin feature/alerts + 4e2fa1b...d19c07a feature/alerts -> feature/alerts (forced update) $ git log --oneline origin/feature/alerts -3 d19c07a Tune alert thresholds $ # Priya&#x27;s commit \"Add runbook link\" is no longer on the branch The force push replaced the branch and dropped a commit that the colleague had pushed a few minutes earlier. The commit was recovered from the colleague&#39;s local clone and the reflog on the server. The team switched to git push --f"},"git/remotes-fetch-pull-and-push":{"html":"\u003cp>A repository on your laptop is only useful to others if it is connected to a shared one. A remote is a named link to another copy of the repository, usually on a server or a code-sharing site. Most daily Git confusion (&quot;why is my push rejected?&quot;, &quot;what did pull just do?&quot;) comes from not seeing that your branches and the remote&#39;s branches are separate things that you synchronize on purpose.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Clone and inspect remotes, and what origin means\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Remote-tracking branches such as origin/main\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The difference between fetch and pull\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Push, upstream branches, rejected pushes and safe forcing\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"clone-and-remotes\">Clone and remotes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git clone git@code.example.com:ops/deploy.git\ncd deploy\ngit remote -v\ngit branch -vv\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">git \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">clone\u003c/span> git@code.example.com:ops/deploy.git\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cd\u003c/span> deploy\ngit remote -v\ngit branch -vv\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>git clone\u003c/code> copies the full history and creates a remote named \u003ccode>origin\u003c/code> pointing to where you cloned from. You can have more than one remote, for example \u003ccode>origin\u003c/code> for your fork and \u003ccode>upstream\u003c/code> for the original project. \u003ccode>git branch -vv\u003c/code> shows each local branch, the remote branch it tracks, and whether you are ahead or behind.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A remote can use SSH (\u003ccode>git@host:path\u003c/code>) or HTTPS (\u003ccode>https://host/path\u003c/code>). SSH uses your key pair and avoids typing passwords; HTTPS usually uses a token stored by a credential helper. Never put a token in the URL of a remote, because it ends up in \u003ccode>.git/config\u003c/code> and shell history.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"remote-tracking-branches\">Remote-tracking branches\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>After a clone, Git keeps a read-only copy of the remote&#39;s branches as \u003ccode>origin/main\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>origin/feature-x\u003c/code> and so on. They only move when you talk to the remote. This is why your local \u003ccode>main\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>origin/main\u003c/code> can differ: one is your work, the other is your last known picture of the server.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git fetch origin            # update origin/* from the server, change nothing else\ngit log --oneline main..origin/main   # commits on the remote that I do not have\ngit log --oneline origin/main..main   # my commits not yet pushed\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">git fetch origin            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># update origin/* from the server, change nothing else\u003c/span>\ngit \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> --oneline main..origin/main   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># commits on the remote that I do not have\u003c/span>\ngit \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> --oneline origin/main..main   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># my commits not yet pushed\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"fetch-versus-pull\">Fetch versus pull\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>git fetch\u003c/code> downloads new commits and updates remote-tracking branches. It never touches your working tree, so it is always safe. \u003ccode>git pull\u003c/code> is \u003ccode>fetch\u003c/code> followed by an integration step: by default a merge, or a rebase if you ask for it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git pull --ff-only      # fail instead of creating a surprise merge commit\ngit pull --rebase       # replay my local commits on top\ngit config --global pull.ff only   # make the safe behaviour the default\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">git pull --ff-only      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># fail instead of creating a surprise merge commit\u003c/span>\ngit pull --rebase       \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># replay my local commits on top\u003c/span>\ngit config --global pull.ff only   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># make the safe behaviour the default\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A habit that avoids surprises: \u003ccode>git fetch\u003c/code>, look at what arrived with \u003ccode>git log\u003c/code>, then integrate. If you see an unexpected merge commit after a pull, it means you and the remote both had new commits.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"push-and-rejected-pushes\">Push and rejected pushes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git push -u origin feature/retry    # first push: create the branch and set its upstream\ngit push                            # later pushes need no arguments\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">git push -u origin feature/retry    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># first push: create the branch and set its upstream\u003c/span>\ngit push                            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># later pushes need no arguments\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A push succeeds only if it is a fast-forward of the remote branch. If someone else pushed first, you see \u003ccode>! [rejected] (non-fast-forward)\u003c/code>. Do not reach for \u003ccode>--force\u003c/code>. Fetch, integrate their commits (rebase or merge), run your tests and push again. If you really rewrote your own feature branch, use \u003ccode>git push --force-with-lease\u003c/code>, which fails if the remote has commits you have not seen. Many teams protect \u003ccode>main\u003c/code> so that nobody, including administrators, can force-push to it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> `git pull` produced a merge commit you did not expect. What does that tell you?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Both your local branch and the remote had new commits, so pull merged them. Use fetch first, or pull --rebase or --ff-only to control it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Pull is fetch plus an integration step.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-push-that-overwrote-a-release-branch\">Incident: The push that overwrote a release branch\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A hurried engineer saw a rejected push and fixed it the quick way.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git push origin release/3.2\ngit push --force origin release/3.2\ngit log --oneline origin/release/3.2 -2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git push origin release/3.2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> ! [rejected]        release/3.2 -&gt; release/3.2 (non-fast-forward)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git push --force origin release/3.2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> + a91c3e0...22d7f10 release/3.2 -&gt; release/3.2 (forced update)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> --oneline origin/release/3.2 -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">22d7f10 Bump version to 3.2.4\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The force push removed two security fix commits from the release branch that a colleague had pushed an hour earlier. The commits were found in the colleague&#39;s clone and restored. The repository now blocks force pushes on release/* and main, and the contributing guide tells people to fetch and rebase when a push is rejected.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Pulling without knowing whether it merges or rebases\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using --force to get past a rejected push\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting access tokens in remote URLs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting to set the upstream so push and pull need arguments\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"clone-and-remotes","text":"Clone and remotes"},{"id":"remote-tracking-branches","text":"Remote-tracking branches"},{"id":"fetch-versus-pull","text":"Fetch versus pull"},{"id":"push-and-rejected-pushes","text":"Push and rejected pushes"},{"id":"incident-the-push-that-overwrote-a-release-branch","text":"Incident: The push that overwrote a release branch"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Pro Git: Working with Remotes","u":"https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Basics-Working-with-Remotes"},{"t":"git-push documentation","u":"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-push"}],"text":"A repository on your laptop is only useful to others if it is connected to a shared one. A remote is a named link to another copy of the repository, usually on a server or a code-sharing site. Most daily Git confusion (\"why is my push rejected?\", \"what did pull just do?\") comes from not seeing that your branches and the remote&#39;s branches are separate things that you synchronize on purpose. What you will learn Clone and inspect remotes, and what origin means Remote-tracking branches such as origin/main The difference between fetch and pull Push, upstream branches, rejected pushes and safe forcing Clone and remotes git clone git@code.example.com:ops/deploy.git cd deploy git remote -v git branch -vv git clone copies the full history and creates a remote named origin pointing to where you cloned from. You can have more than one remote, for example origin for your fork and upstream for the original project. git branch -vv shows each local branch, the remote branch it tracks, and whether you are ahead or behind. A remote can use SSH ( git@host:path ) or HTTPS ( https://host/path ). SSH uses your key pair and avoids typing passwords; HTTPS usually uses a token stored by a credential helper. Never put a token in the URL of a remote, because it ends up in .git/config and shell history. Remote-tracking branches After a clone, Git keeps a read-only copy of the remote&#39;s branches as origin/main , origin/feature-x and so on. They only move when you talk to the remote. This is why your local main and origin/main can differ: one is your work, the other is your last known picture of the server. git fetch origin # update origin/* from the server, change nothing else git log --oneline main..origin/main # commits on the remote that I do not have git log --oneline origin/main..main # my commits not yet pushed Fetch versus pull git fetch downloads new commits and updates remote-tracking branches. It never touches your working tree, so it is always safe. git pull is fetch followed by an integration step: by default a merge, or a rebase if you ask for it. git pull --ff-only # fail instead of creating a surprise merge commit git pull --rebase # replay my local commits on top git config --global pull.ff only # make the safe behaviour the default A habit that avoids surprises: git fetch , look at what arrived with git log , then integrate. If you see an unexpected merge commit after a pull, it means you and the remote both had new commits. Push and rejected pushes git push -u origin feature/retry # first push: create the branch and set its upstream git push # later pushes need no arguments A push succeeds only if it is a fast-forward of the remote branch. If someone else pushed first, you see ! [rejected] (non-fast-forward) . Do not reach for --force . Fetch, integrate their commits (rebase or merge), run your tests and push again. If you really rewrote your own feature branch, use git push --force-with-lease , which fails if the remote has commits you have not seen. Many teams protect main so that nobody, including administrators, can force-push to it. Predict first. `git pull` produced a merge commit you did not expect. What does that tell you? Your guess Answer: Both your local branch and the remote had new commits, so pull merged them. Use fetch first, or pull --rebase or --ff-only to control it. Pull is fetch plus an integration step. Incident: The push that overwrote a release branch Incident A hurried engineer saw a rejected push and fixed it the quick way. $ git push origin release/3.2 ! [rejected] release/3.2 -> release/3.2 (non-fast-forward) $ git push --force origin release/3.2 + a91c3e0...22d7f10 release/3.2 -> release/3.2 (forced update) $ git log --oneline origin/release/3.2 -2 22d7f10 Bump version to 3.2.4 The force push removed two security fix commits from the release branch that a colleague had pushed an hour earlier. The commits were found in the colleague&#39;s clone and restored. The repository now blocks force pushes on rel"},"git/team-workflows-pull-requests-and-reviews":{"html":"\u003cp>Git does not tell you how a team should work. Without agreed rules, five people push to main in five styles, reviews are rare, and releases are tense. A workflow is a small set of rules about branches, reviews and merges. The best ones are boring: short branches, small changes, automatic checks, and a main branch that is always releasable.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Compare trunk-based development, feature branches and release branches\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Write pull requests that are easy to review\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Protect main with required reviews and status checks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use CODEOWNERS and clear commit messages\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"three-common-strategies\">Three common strategies\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Trunk-based development\u003c/strong>: everyone merges small changes to \u003ccode>main\u003c/code> at least daily, often behind feature flags. It needs good tests and a fast pipeline, and gives the least merge pain. \u003cstrong>Feature branches\u003c/strong> (a simple flow that works with any hosting): one short branch per change, reviewed through a pull request, then merged to \u003ccode>main\u003c/code> and deployed. \u003cstrong>Release branches\u003c/strong> (as in Gitflow): long-lived \u003ccode>develop\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>release/*\u003c/code> branches, useful when you must support several released versions at once, but slower and more complex. For web services and infrastructure code, trunk-based or short feature branches usually fit best; for installed software with several supported versions, release branches earn their cost.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-pull-request-people-can-review\">A pull request people can review\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A pull request (also called a merge request) proposes merging one branch into another and is the place for discussion, review and automated checks. Good habits:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Keep it small: a few hundred changed lines at most. Large changes get skimmed, not reviewed.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Write what and why in the description, how you tested it, and link the ticket.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Separate refactoring from behaviour changes.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Answer comments with a commit or an explanation, and re-request review.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Reviewers should read for correctness, safety (what happens when this fails?), clarity and tests, and say which comments are blocking and which are suggestions. For infrastructure code, attach the plan output, because the diff of code does not show what will be created or destroyed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"protect-the-main-branch\">Protect the main branch\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Hosting platforms let you set rules on \u003ccode>main\u003c/code> that nobody can bypass by accident:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"Require a pull request before merging\nRequire at least 1 approving review (2 for production config)\nRequire status checks: lint, unit tests, security scan\nRequire branches to be up to date before merging\nBlock force pushes and branch deletion\nRequire signed commits (optional)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">Require a pull request before merging\nRequire at least 1 approving review (2 for production config)\nRequire status checks: lint, unit tests, security scan\nRequire branches to be up to date before merging\nBlock force pushes and branch deletion\nRequire signed commits (optional)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The checks must run on the pull request, and the merge button stays disabled until they pass. A \u003ccode>CODEOWNERS\u003c/code> file maps paths to teams, so a change under \u003ccode>deploy/prod/\u003c/code> automatically requests review from the platform team:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"/deploy/prod/   @acme/platform-team\n*.tf            @acme/infra-reviewers\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">/deploy/prod/   @acme/platform-team\n*.tf            @acme/infra-reviewers\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"commit-and-merge-conventions\">Commit and merge conventions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Pick one merge style (squash, rebase or merge commit) and use it consistently. Many teams adopt a message convention such as \u003ccode>type(scope): summary\u003c/code> (for example \u003ccode>fix(backup): retry on timeout\u003c/code>), because tools can then build changelogs and decide version numbers. Keep the first line under about 72 characters. The point of all these rules is the same: history that a stranger can read at 03:00 during an incident.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A pull request changes 2,400 lines across 40 files. What is the practical problem and the usual remedy?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Reviewers cannot review it properly. Split it into smaller pull requests, separating refactoring from behaviour changes.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Review quality falls quickly as size grows.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-unreviewed-hotfix\">Incident: The unreviewed hotfix\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A late-night fix was pushed straight to main because the rule was only a convention.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git log --oneline -3 main\ngit log -1 --format=%an,%cd 7fa90cd\ncurl -s https://shop.example.com/health\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> --oneline -3 main\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">7fa90cd Hotfix: raise db pool size\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> -1 --format=%an,%\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cd\u003c/span> 7fa90cd\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">sam, Thu Jan 5 23:48\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s https://shop.example.com/health\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{&quot;db&quot;:&quot;error: too many connections&quot;}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The fix raised the connection pool beyond what the database allowed and took the service down at the next restart. Nobody had reviewed it because direct pushes to main were possible. After the incident the team enabled branch protection with required review and checks, and added an expedited path: a pull request that needs one reviewer and runs the fast checks, so an urgent fix is still reviewed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Allowing direct pushes to main\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Huge pull requests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Required checks that are flaky, so people bypass them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Long-lived branches with unclear owners\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"three-common-strategies","text":"Three common strategies"},{"id":"a-pull-request-people-can-review","text":"A pull request people can review"},{"id":"protect-the-main-branch","text":"Protect the main branch"},{"id":"commit-and-merge-conventions","text":"Commit and merge conventions"},{"id":"incident-the-unreviewed-hotfix","text":"Incident: The unreviewed hotfix"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Atlassian: Comparing Git workflows","u":"https://www.atlassian.com/git/tutorials/comparing-workflows"},{"t":"Martin Fowler: Patterns for Managing Source Code Branches","u":"https://martinfowler.com/articles/branching-patterns.html"}],"text":"Git does not tell you how a team should work. Without agreed rules, five people push to main in five styles, reviews are rare, and releases are tense. A workflow is a small set of rules about branches, reviews and merges. The best ones are boring: short branches, small changes, automatic checks, and a main branch that is always releasable. What you will learn Compare trunk-based development, feature branches and release branches Write pull requests that are easy to review Protect main with required reviews and status checks Use CODEOWNERS and clear commit messages Three common strategies Trunk-based development : everyone merges small changes to main at least daily, often behind feature flags. It needs good tests and a fast pipeline, and gives the least merge pain. Feature branches (a simple flow that works with any hosting): one short branch per change, reviewed through a pull request, then merged to main and deployed. Release branches (as in Gitflow): long-lived develop and release/* branches, useful when you must support several released versions at once, but slower and more complex. For web services and infrastructure code, trunk-based or short feature branches usually fit best; for installed software with several supported versions, release branches earn their cost. A pull request people can review A pull request (also called a merge request) proposes merging one branch into another and is the place for discussion, review and automated checks. Good habits: Keep it small: a few hundred changed lines at most. Large changes get skimmed, not reviewed. Write what and why in the description, how you tested it, and link the ticket. Separate refactoring from behaviour changes. Answer comments with a commit or an explanation, and re-request review. Reviewers should read for correctness, safety (what happens when this fails?), clarity and tests, and say which comments are blocking and which are suggestions. For infrastructure code, attach the plan output, because the diff of code does not show what will be created or destroyed. Protect the main branch Hosting platforms let you set rules on main that nobody can bypass by accident: Require a pull request before merging Require at least 1 approving review (2 for production config) Require status checks: lint, unit tests, security scan Require branches to be up to date before merging Block force pushes and branch deletion Require signed commits (optional) The checks must run on the pull request, and the merge button stays disabled until they pass. A CODEOWNERS file maps paths to teams, so a change under deploy/prod/ automatically requests review from the platform team: /deploy/prod/ @acme/platform-team *.tf @acme/infra-reviewers Commit and merge conventions Pick one merge style (squash, rebase or merge commit) and use it consistently. Many teams adopt a message convention such as type(scope): summary (for example fix(backup): retry on timeout ), because tools can then build changelogs and decide version numbers. Keep the first line under about 72 characters. The point of all these rules is the same: history that a stranger can read at 03:00 during an incident. Predict first. A pull request changes 2,400 lines across 40 files. What is the practical problem and the usual remedy? Your guess Answer: Reviewers cannot review it properly. Split it into smaller pull requests, separating refactoring from behaviour changes. Review quality falls quickly as size grows. Incident: The unreviewed hotfix Incident A late-night fix was pushed straight to main because the rule was only a convention. $ git log --oneline -3 main 7fa90cd Hotfix: raise db pool size $ git log -1 --format=%an,% cd 7fa90cd sam, Thu Jan 5 23:48 $ curl -s https://shop.example.com/health {\"db\":\"error: too many connections\"} The fix raised the connection pool beyond what the database allowed and took the service down at the next restart. Nobody had reviewed it because direct pushes to main were possible. After the incident the team"},"git/tags-releases-and-versioning":{"html":"\u003cp>In production, someone asks, &quot;which exact code is running?&quot; A branch name is no answer, because branches move. A \u003cstrong>tag\u003c/strong> is a permanent name for one commit, and a version number on a tag is how teams, pipelines and customers talk about releases. Getting tags and versions right makes rollbacks, audits and incident analysis much simpler.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Lightweight and annotated tags, and why releases use annotated ones\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Semantic versioning: MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Release branches and hotfixes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Changelogs and finding what is in a release\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tags\">Tags\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A tag is a fixed name for a commit. A \u003cstrong>lightweight\u003c/strong> tag is only a pointer. An \u003cstrong>annotated\u003c/strong> tag is a full object with a tagger, date and message, and can be signed. Use annotated tags for releases.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git tag -a v1.4.0 -m &quot;Release 1.4.0: add retry to backup job&quot;\ngit tag -s v1.4.1 -m &quot;Hotfix, signed&quot;     # signed with your key\ngit push origin v1.4.0                      # tags are not pushed by default\ngit push origin --tags\ngit show v1.4.0\ngit describe --tags                         # e.g. v1.4.0-12-g3f9c2ab\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">git tag -a v1.4.0 -m \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Release 1.4.0: add retry to backup job&quot;\u003c/span>\ngit tag -s v1.4.1 -m \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Hotfix, signed&quot;\u003c/span>     \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># signed with your key\u003c/span>\ngit push origin v1.4.0                      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># tags are not pushed by default\u003c/span>\ngit push origin --tags\ngit show v1.4.0\ngit describe --tags                         \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># e.g. v1.4.0-12-g3f9c2ab\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>git describe\u003c/code> turns any commit into a readable name: the last tag, how many commits since, and the short hash. Many pipelines use it to stamp the version into builds. Do not move or delete a published tag; deployed artifacts already refer to it. If a release is wrong, publish a new version.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"semantic-versioning\">Semantic versioning\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Semantic versioning (SemVer) uses \u003ccode>MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>PATCH\u003c/strong> for backward-compatible bug fixes (1.4.0 to 1.4.1)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>MINOR\u003c/strong> for backward-compatible new features (1.4.1 to 1.5.0)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>MAJOR\u003c/strong> for breaking changes (1.5.0 to 2.0.0)\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>A pre-release is written \u003ccode>2.0.0-rc.1\u003c/code>. The numbers are a promise to the people who depend on you: a minor upgrade should not break them. Services that nobody installs themselves sometimes use dates (\u003ccode>2026.01.05\u003c/code>) or build numbers; choose one scheme and write it down.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"releases-and-hotfixes\">Releases and hotfixes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A simple flow: develop on short branches, merge to \u003ccode>main\u003c/code>, tag the release commit, build the artifact from that tag, and deploy the same artifact everywhere. If you need to fix an old version, branch from its tag:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git switch -c hotfix/1.4 v1.4.0\n# fix, commit, then\ngit tag -a v1.4.1 -m &quot;Fix backup path&quot;\ngit switch main &amp;&amp; git cherry-pick HASH   # bring the fix forward\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">git switch -c hotfix/1.4 v1.4.0\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># fix, commit, then\u003c/span>\ngit tag -a v1.4.1 -m \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Fix backup path&quot;\u003c/span>\ngit switch main &amp;&amp; git cherry-pick HASH   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># bring the fix forward\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>git cherry-pick\u003c/code> copies one commit onto another branch. Always make sure the fix also reaches \u003ccode>main\u003c/code>, or the bug returns in the next release.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"changelogs-and-questions-you-can-answer\">Changelogs and questions you can answer\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Keep a human-written changelog that lists notable changes per version under headings such as Added, Changed, Fixed and Security. Generating it from commit messages works if messages are disciplined. Tags let you answer production questions quickly:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git log --oneline v1.3.0..v1.4.0       # what is in this release\ngit tag --contains 3f9c2ab              # which releases contain this fix\ngit diff v1.4.0 v1.4.1 --stat           # what the hotfix changed\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">git \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> --oneline v1.3.0..v1.4.0       \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># what is in this release\u003c/span>\ngit tag --contains 3f9c2ab              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># which releases contain this fix\u003c/span>\ngit diff v1.4.0 v1.4.1 --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">stat\u003c/span>           \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># what the hotfix changed\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A customer on 1.4.0 reports a bug fixed in a commit on main. How do you find which released versions contain the fix?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Use git tag --contains COMMIT to list the tags that include that commit.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Tags make &quot;is it in this release?&quot; a one-line question.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-tag-that-moved\">Incident: The tag that moved\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Two builds both said version 2.1.0, and they behaved differently.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git rev-parse v2.1.0\ngit ls-remote --tags origin v2.1.0\ndocker image inspect shop:2.1.0 --format &quot;{{index .Config.Labels \\&quot;commit\\&quot;}}&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git rev-parse v2.1.0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">9e0b7c1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git ls-remote --tags origin v2.1.0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">4c18aa2    refs/tags/v2.1.0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker image inspect shop:2.1.0 --format \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;{{index .Config.Labels \\&quot;commit\\&quot;}}&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">9e0b7c1\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A developer had deleted and recreated the tag v2.1.0 on a different commit after a late fix. Production still ran the image built from the first commit, while the new tag pointed to different code. The team banned moving tags (the server now rejects tag updates), started stamping the commit hash into every image, and decided that a mistake means releasing 2.1.1.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Moving or deleting published tags\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Lightweight tags for releases\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Breaking changes in a minor version\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Hotfixes that never reach main\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"tags","text":"Tags"},{"id":"semantic-versioning","text":"Semantic versioning"},{"id":"releases-and-hotfixes","text":"Releases and hotfixes"},{"id":"changelogs-and-questions-you-can-answer","text":"Changelogs and questions you can answer"},{"id":"incident-the-tag-that-moved","text":"Incident: The tag that moved"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Pro Git: Tagging","u":"https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Basics-Tagging"},{"t":"Semantic Versioning 2.0.0","u":"https://semver.org/"}],"text":"In production, someone asks, \"which exact code is running?\" A branch name is no answer, because branches move. A tag is a permanent name for one commit, and a version number on a tag is how teams, pipelines and customers talk about releases. Getting tags and versions right makes rollbacks, audits and incident analysis much simpler. What you will learn Lightweight and annotated tags, and why releases use annotated ones Semantic versioning: MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH Release branches and hotfixes Changelogs and finding what is in a release Tags A tag is a fixed name for a commit. A lightweight tag is only a pointer. An annotated tag is a full object with a tagger, date and message, and can be signed. Use annotated tags for releases. git tag -a v1.4.0 -m \"Release 1.4.0: add retry to backup job\" git tag -s v1.4.1 -m \"Hotfix, signed\" # signed with your key git push origin v1.4.0 # tags are not pushed by default git push origin --tags git show v1.4.0 git describe --tags # e.g. v1.4.0-12-g3f9c2ab git describe turns any commit into a readable name: the last tag, how many commits since, and the short hash. Many pipelines use it to stamp the version into builds. Do not move or delete a published tag; deployed artifacts already refer to it. If a release is wrong, publish a new version. Semantic versioning Semantic versioning (SemVer) uses MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH : PATCH for backward-compatible bug fixes (1.4.0 to 1.4.1) MINOR for backward-compatible new features (1.4.1 to 1.5.0) MAJOR for breaking changes (1.5.0 to 2.0.0) A pre-release is written 2.0.0-rc.1 . The numbers are a promise to the people who depend on you: a minor upgrade should not break them. Services that nobody installs themselves sometimes use dates ( 2026.01.05 ) or build numbers; choose one scheme and write it down. Releases and hotfixes A simple flow: develop on short branches, merge to main , tag the release commit, build the artifact from that tag, and deploy the same artifact everywhere. If you need to fix an old version, branch from its tag: git switch -c hotfix/1.4 v1.4.0 # fix, commit, then git tag -a v1.4.1 -m \"Fix backup path\" git switch main && git cherry-pick HASH # bring the fix forward git cherry-pick copies one commit onto another branch. Always make sure the fix also reaches main , or the bug returns in the next release. Changelogs and questions you can answer Keep a human-written changelog that lists notable changes per version under headings such as Added, Changed, Fixed and Security. Generating it from commit messages works if messages are disciplined. Tags let you answer production questions quickly: git log --oneline v1.3.0..v1.4.0 # what is in this release git tag --contains 3f9c2ab # which releases contain this fix git diff v1.4.0 v1.4.1 -- stat # what the hotfix changed Predict first. A customer on 1.4.0 reports a bug fixed in a commit on main. How do you find which released versions contain the fix? Your guess Answer: Use git tag --contains COMMIT to list the tags that include that commit. Tags make \"is it in this release?\" a one-line question. Incident: The tag that moved Incident Two builds both said version 2.1.0, and they behaved differently. $ git rev-parse v2.1.0 9e0b7c1 $ git ls-remote --tags origin v2.1.0 4c18aa2 refs/tags/v2.1.0 $ docker image inspect shop:2.1.0 --format \"{{index .Config.Labels \\\"commit\\\"}}\" 9e0b7c1 A developer had deleted and recreated the tag v2.1.0 on a different commit after a late fix. Production still ran the image built from the first commit, while the new tag pointed to different code. The team banned moving tags (the server now rejects tag updates), started stamping the commit hash into every image, and decided that a mistake means releasing 2.1.1. Common mistakes Moving or deleting published tags Lightweight tags for releases Breaking changes in a minor version Hotfixes that never reach main"},"git/hooks-signing-and-secrets-in-history":{"html":"\u003cp>A developer commits a cloud access key by accident. Deleting the file in the next commit does not help, because Git keeps the old version in history and anyone with a clone has it. This lesson covers prevention (hooks and scanning), proof of authorship (signing) and the correct, ordered response when prevention fails. The order matters: revoke first, clean later.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Client-side hooks and the pre-commit approach\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Server-side protection and secret scanning\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Signed commits and tags\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Responding to a leaked secret: revoke, rewrite, notify\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"hooks\">Hooks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Hooks are scripts that Git runs at certain moments. They live in \u003ccode>.git/hooks\u003c/code>, which is not shared by clone, so teams manage them with a tool or a shared directory.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# .git/hooks/pre-commit  (must be executable)\n#!/bin/sh\nif git diff --cached --name-only | grep -E &quot;\\.env$|id_rsa$&quot;; then\n  echo &quot;Refusing to commit a secret file&quot;; exit 1\nfi\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># .git/hooks/pre-commit  (must be executable)\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">#!/bin/sh\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> git diff --cached --name-only | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\\.env$|id_rsa$&quot;\u003c/span>; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">then\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Refusing to commit a secret file&quot;\u003c/span>; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exit\u003c/span> 1\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">fi\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Common hooks: \u003ccode>pre-commit\u003c/code> (lint, format, scan), \u003ccode>commit-msg\u003c/code> (check the message format) and \u003ccode>pre-push\u003c/code> (run fast tests). The \u003ccode>pre-commit\u003c/code> framework lets you list hooks in a \u003ccode>.pre-commit-config.yaml\u003c/code> file that everyone installs with one command. Hooks can be skipped with \u003ccode>--no-verify\u003c/code>, so they help honest people, and the real enforcement belongs on the server and in the pipeline.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"server-side-protection\">Server-side protection\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run the same checks in the pipeline on every pull request, and turn on \u003cstrong>push protection\u003c/strong> or secret scanning on the hosting platform so that known token formats are blocked or reported. Add a scanner such as a secrets-detection tool to the pipeline, scan the full history periodically, and treat any finding as an incident. Keep real secrets in a secret manager and use short-lived credentials, so a leak is less damaging.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"signed-commits\">Signed commits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Anyone can set \u003ccode>user.name\u003c/code> to anything, so the author line proves nothing. A signature proves the commit came from the holder of a key. Git can sign with GPG or, more simply, with an SSH key:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git config --global gpg.format ssh\ngit config --global user.signingkey ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub\ngit config --global commit.gpgsign true\ngit log --show-signature -1\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">git config --global gpg.format ssh\ngit config --global user.signingkey ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub\ngit config --global commit.gpgsign \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\ngit \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> --show-signature -1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The hosting platform shows a &quot;verified&quot; badge when it knows your public key, and a branch rule can require signed commits. Signing matters most for release tags and for repositories that deploy automatically.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-secret-was-committed-the-order-of-actions\">A secret was committed: the order of actions\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Revoke and rotate the secret immediately.\u003c/strong> Assume it is already copied. Rewriting history without revoking leaves a working credential in the wild.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Check logs for use of the secret during the exposure.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Remove it from history if needed, using a rewriting tool such as \u003ccode>git filter-repo\u003c/code>:\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git filter-repo --path config/prod.env --invert-paths\ngit push --force-with-lease --all\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">git filter-repo --path config/prod.env --invert-paths\ngit push --force-with-lease --all\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003col start=\"4\">\n\u003cli>Ask everyone to re-clone or reset, because old clones and forks still contain it. Ask the hosting provider to purge cached views of the old commits.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Add a prevention (hook, push protection, ignore rule) and write a short review of how it happened.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Cleaning history is secondary; the credential must be dead before you start.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You delete a committed password file and push. Is the password safe?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> No. It remains in history and in every clone. You must rotate the password first, then optionally rewrite history.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Deleting in a later commit does not erase the earlier version.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-key-in-the-config-file\">Incident: The key in the config file\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A scanner raised an alert on a repository that had been public for three days.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git log --all --oneline -S&quot;AKIA&quot; -- config/\naws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=AccessKeyId,AttributeValue=AKIAEXAMPLE --max-results 3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> --all --oneline -S\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;AKIA&quot;\u003c/span> -- config/\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">5b2c9de Add backup settings\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=AccessKeyId,AttributeValue=AKIAEXAMPLE --max-results 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">RunInstances   2026-01-04T02:11:09Z  region ap-south-1\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>An access key was in a settings file, and someone had used it to start compute instances. The team revoked the key within minutes, terminated the instances and then rewrote the history. They installed push protection, added a pre-commit scan, moved secrets to a secret manager and made the CI role assumable with short-lived credentials so no static key exists to leak.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Rotating nothing and only deleting the file\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Relying on a local hook that can be skipped\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rewriting history without telling the team\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using shared long-lived keys in repositories\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"hooks","text":"Hooks"},{"id":"server-side-protection","text":"Server-side protection"},{"id":"signed-commits","text":"Signed commits"},{"id":"a-secret-was-committed-the-order-of-actions","text":"A secret was committed: the order of actions"},{"id":"incident-the-key-in-the-config-file","text":"Incident: The key in the config file"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Pro Git: Git Hooks","u":"https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Customizing-Git-Git-Hooks"},{"t":"Pro Git: Signing Your Work","u":"https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Tools-Signing-Your-Work"}],"text":"A developer commits a cloud access key by accident. Deleting the file in the next commit does not help, because Git keeps the old version in history and anyone with a clone has it. This lesson covers prevention (hooks and scanning), proof of authorship (signing) and the correct, ordered response when prevention fails. The order matters: revoke first, clean later. What you will learn Client-side hooks and the pre-commit approach Server-side protection and secret scanning Signed commits and tags Responding to a leaked secret: revoke, rewrite, notify Hooks Hooks are scripts that Git runs at certain moments. They live in .git/hooks , which is not shared by clone, so teams manage them with a tool or a shared directory. # .git/hooks/pre-commit (must be executable) #!/bin/sh if git diff --cached --name-only | grep -E \"\\.env$|id_rsa$\" ; then echo \"Refusing to commit a secret file\" ; exit 1 fi Common hooks: pre-commit (lint, format, scan), commit-msg (check the message format) and pre-push (run fast tests). The pre-commit framework lets you list hooks in a .pre-commit-config.yaml file that everyone installs with one command. Hooks can be skipped with --no-verify , so they help honest people, and the real enforcement belongs on the server and in the pipeline. Server-side protection Run the same checks in the pipeline on every pull request, and turn on push protection or secret scanning on the hosting platform so that known token formats are blocked or reported. Add a scanner such as a secrets-detection tool to the pipeline, scan the full history periodically, and treat any finding as an incident. Keep real secrets in a secret manager and use short-lived credentials, so a leak is less damaging. Signed commits Anyone can set user.name to anything, so the author line proves nothing. A signature proves the commit came from the holder of a key. Git can sign with GPG or, more simply, with an SSH key: git config --global gpg.format ssh git config --global user.signingkey ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub git config --global commit.gpgsign true git log --show-signature -1 The hosting platform shows a \"verified\" badge when it knows your public key, and a branch rule can require signed commits. Signing matters most for release tags and for repositories that deploy automatically. A secret was committed: the order of actions Revoke and rotate the secret immediately. Assume it is already copied. Rewriting history without revoking leaves a working credential in the wild. Check logs for use of the secret during the exposure. Remove it from history if needed, using a rewriting tool such as git filter-repo : git filter-repo --path config/prod.env --invert-paths git push --force-with-lease --all Ask everyone to re-clone or reset, because old clones and forks still contain it. Ask the hosting provider to purge cached views of the old commits. Add a prevention (hook, push protection, ignore rule) and write a short review of how it happened. Cleaning history is secondary; the credential must be dead before you start. Predict first. You delete a committed password file and push. Is the password safe? Your guess Answer: No. It remains in history and in every clone. You must rotate the password first, then optionally rewrite history. Deleting in a later commit does not erase the earlier version. Incident: The key in the config file Incident A scanner raised an alert on a repository that had been public for three days. $ git log --all --oneline -S \"AKIA\" -- config/ 5b2c9de Add backup settings $ aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=AccessKeyId,AttributeValue=AKIAEXAMPLE --max-results 3 RunInstances 2026-01-04T02:11:09Z region ap-south-1 An access key was in a settings file, and someone had used it to start compute instances. The team revoked the key within minutes, terminated the instances and then rewrote the history. They installed push protection, added a pre-commit scan, moved secrets to a secret manager and made the CI role assumable with short"},"git/large-repositories-submodules-and-worktrees":{"html":"\u003cp>Repositories grow. A project that cloned in seconds takes minutes after years of history, large binaries and many contributors. CI jobs clone on every run, so slow clones become slow pipelines and real money. Git has several tools to keep large repositories usable, and a few ways to combine repositories. Knowing which to pick, and which to avoid, saves many headaches.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Shallow, partial and sparse clones for speed\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Large files with Git LFS, and when to keep binaries out\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Submodules and alternatives for sharing code\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Worktrees: several branches checked out at once\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"clone-less\">Clone less\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Not every job needs the full history.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git clone --depth 1 URL                    # shallow: only the latest commit\ngit clone --filter=blob:none URL           # partial: fetch file contents on demand\ngit clone --filter=blob:none --sparse URL\ncd repo &amp;&amp; git sparse-checkout set services/payments docs\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">git \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">clone\u003c/span> --depth 1 URL                    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># shallow: only the latest commit\u003c/span>\ngit \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">clone\u003c/span> --filter=blob:none URL           \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># partial: fetch file contents on demand\u003c/span>\ngit \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">clone\u003c/span> --filter=blob:none --sparse URL\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cd\u003c/span> repo &amp;&amp; git sparse-checkout \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> services/payments docs\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>shallow clone\u003c/strong> is ideal for CI builds that only need the current files, but commands that need history (\u003ccode>git describe\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>git log\u003c/code>, blame, some versioning tools) will not work, so deepen it with \u003ccode>git fetch --deepen=50\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>--unshallow\u003c/code>. A \u003cstrong>partial clone\u003c/strong> keeps all commits but downloads file contents only when needed. \u003cstrong>Sparse checkout\u003c/strong> limits the working tree to chosen folders, which is how huge monorepos stay manageable.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"large-files\">Large files\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Git stores every version of every file. A 200 MB binary changed ten times is 2 GB of history. Options:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Keep build artifacts and large data \u003cstrong>out\u003c/strong> of Git; use an artifact registry or object storage and record the version in a file.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>For binaries that must live with the code (design files, test data), use \u003cstrong>Git LFS\u003c/strong>: Git stores a small pointer, and the large content goes to a separate store.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git lfs install\ngit lfs track &quot;*.psd&quot;\ngit add .gitattributes\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">git lfs install\ngit lfs track \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;*.psd&quot;\u003c/span>\ngit add .gitattributes\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The tracking rules are saved in \u003ccode>.gitattributes\u003c/code>. Decide before the files are committed; moving existing large files to LFS needs a history rewrite. Run \u003ccode>git count-objects -vH\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>git rev-list --objects --all | sort -k2 | head\u003c/code> style checks, or a size report in CI, to notice growth early.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"submodules-and-alternatives\">Submodules and alternatives\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>submodule\u003c/strong> embeds another repository at a fixed commit inside yours:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git submodule add https://code.example.com/ops/shared-scripts.git vendor/scripts\ngit clone --recurse-submodules URL\ngit submodule update --init --recursive\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">git submodule add https://code.example.com/ops/shared-scripts.git vendor/scripts\ngit \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">clone\u003c/span> --recurse-submodules URL\ngit submodule update --init --recursive\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The parent records an exact commit of the child, which is good for reproducibility and a common source of confusion: people forget to update the submodule, commit in a detached state, or clone without \u003ccode>--recurse-submodules\u003c/code> and get empty folders. Alternatives are a package manager (publish the shared code as a versioned package), \u003ccode>git subtree\u003c/code> (copies the code into your history), or a monorepo. Prefer packages for libraries; use submodules when you truly need a pinned copy of another repository.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"worktrees\">Worktrees\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Switching branches in the middle of work means stashing or committing. A \u003cstrong>worktree\u003c/strong> gives you another folder with a different branch checked out, sharing the same repository data:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git worktree add ../hotfix-1.4 hotfix/1.4\ncd ../hotfix-1.4\n# fix and test while the main folder keeps your feature work\ngit worktree list\ngit worktree remove ../hotfix-1.4\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">git worktree add ../hotfix-1.4 hotfix/1.4\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cd\u003c/span> ../hotfix-1.4\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># fix and test while the main folder keeps your feature work\u003c/span>\ngit worktree list\ngit worktree remove ../hotfix-1.4\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This is handy for urgent fixes, reviewing a pull request in a clean directory, and running long tests on one branch while you code on another. Housekeeping runs automatically, and \u003ccode>git maintenance start\u003c/code> can schedule background optimization for large repositories.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A CI job only needs to build the current code, but the clone takes 6 minutes. Which options help?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> A shallow clone (--depth 1), a partial clone, or sparse checkout of just the needed folders.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Do not fetch history the job never uses.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-pipeline-that-cloned-for-eleven-minutes\">Incident: The pipeline that cloned for eleven minutes\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Builds became slower each month, and a pipeline run spent most of its time before the first build step.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"time git clone ssh://code.example.com/platform/mono.git\ntime git clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none ssh://code.example.com/platform/mono.git\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">time\u003c/span> git \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">clone\u003c/span> ssh://code.example.com/platform/mono.git\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Receiving objects: 100% (3604211/3604211), 7.4 GiB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">real 11m02s\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">time\u003c/span> git \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">clone\u003c/span> --depth 1 --filter=blob:none ssh://code.example.com/platform/mono.git\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">real 0m19s\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The job needed only the files of one service. The team changed the pipeline to use a shallow, partial clone with sparse checkout of the service folder and its shared libraries, which cut clone time to under 20 seconds. They also moved old release bundles out of the repository and added a size alert so growth is noticed early.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Committing large binaries without LFS or an artifact store\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cloning full history in every CI job\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting --recurse-submodules and building with empty folders\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Editing submodule contents in a detached state and losing the commit\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"clone-less","text":"Clone less"},{"id":"large-files","text":"Large files"},{"id":"submodules-and-alternatives","text":"Submodules and alternatives"},{"id":"worktrees","text":"Worktrees"},{"id":"incident-the-pipeline-that-cloned-for-eleven-minutes","text":"Incident: The pipeline that cloned for eleven minutes"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"git-clone documentation","u":"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-clone"},{"t":"Pro Git: Submodules","u":"https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Tools-Submodules"}],"text":"Repositories grow. A project that cloned in seconds takes minutes after years of history, large binaries and many contributors. CI jobs clone on every run, so slow clones become slow pipelines and real money. Git has several tools to keep large repositories usable, and a few ways to combine repositories. Knowing which to pick, and which to avoid, saves many headaches. What you will learn Shallow, partial and sparse clones for speed Large files with Git LFS, and when to keep binaries out Submodules and alternatives for sharing code Worktrees: several branches checked out at once Clone less Not every job needs the full history. git clone --depth 1 URL # shallow: only the latest commit git clone --filter=blob:none URL # partial: fetch file contents on demand git clone --filter=blob:none --sparse URL cd repo && git sparse-checkout set services/payments docs A shallow clone is ideal for CI builds that only need the current files, but commands that need history ( git describe , git log , blame, some versioning tools) will not work, so deepen it with git fetch --deepen=50 or --unshallow . A partial clone keeps all commits but downloads file contents only when needed. Sparse checkout limits the working tree to chosen folders, which is how huge monorepos stay manageable. Large files Git stores every version of every file. A 200 MB binary changed ten times is 2 GB of history. Options: Keep build artifacts and large data out of Git; use an artifact registry or object storage and record the version in a file. For binaries that must live with the code (design files, test data), use Git LFS : Git stores a small pointer, and the large content goes to a separate store. git lfs install git lfs track \"*.psd\" git add .gitattributes The tracking rules are saved in .gitattributes . Decide before the files are committed; moving existing large files to LFS needs a history rewrite. Run git count-objects -vH and git rev-list --objects --all | sort -k2 | head style checks, or a size report in CI, to notice growth early. Submodules and alternatives A submodule embeds another repository at a fixed commit inside yours: git submodule add https://code.example.com/ops/shared-scripts.git vendor/scripts git clone --recurse-submodules URL git submodule update --init --recursive The parent records an exact commit of the child, which is good for reproducibility and a common source of confusion: people forget to update the submodule, commit in a detached state, or clone without --recurse-submodules and get empty folders. Alternatives are a package manager (publish the shared code as a versioned package), git subtree (copies the code into your history), or a monorepo. Prefer packages for libraries; use submodules when you truly need a pinned copy of another repository. Worktrees Switching branches in the middle of work means stashing or committing. A worktree gives you another folder with a different branch checked out, sharing the same repository data: git worktree add ../hotfix-1.4 hotfix/1.4 cd ../hotfix-1.4 # fix and test while the main folder keeps your feature work git worktree list git worktree remove ../hotfix-1.4 This is handy for urgent fixes, reviewing a pull request in a clean directory, and running long tests on one branch while you code on another. Housekeeping runs automatically, and git maintenance start can schedule background optimization for large repositories. Predict first. A CI job only needs to build the current code, but the clone takes 6 minutes. Which options help? Your guess Answer: A shallow clone (--depth 1), a partial clone, or sparse checkout of just the needed folders. Do not fetch history the job never uses. Incident: The pipeline that cloned for eleven minutes Incident Builds became slower each month, and a pipeline run spent most of its time before the first build step. $ time git clone ssh://code.example.com/platform/mono.git Receiving objects: 100% (3604211/3604211), 7.4 GiB real 11m02s $ time git clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:n"},"git/debugging-git-problems":{"html":"\u003cp>At some point Git will show you something frightening: &quot;detached HEAD&quot;, a branch that vanished, a commit you cannot find, a build that broke between two releases. In nearly every case the data is still there. Git almost never deletes committed work immediately, and it also keeps a private journal of where your branches pointed. Knowing how to read that journal and how to search history turns panic into a routine investigation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Find and recover lost commits with git reflog\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Locate the commit that introduced a bug with git bisect\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Answer who changed this and why with blame and log -S\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Understand detached HEAD, wrong-branch commits and unfinished operations\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"recover-with-the-reflog\">Recover with the reflog\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>reflog\u003c/strong> records every time \u003ccode>HEAD\u003c/code> or a branch moved on your machine: commits, resets, rebases, checkouts. It is local and expires after weeks, but it is the safety net for almost every &quot;I lost my work&quot; moment.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git reflog -15\n# 41ac9d2 HEAD@{0}: reset: moving to origin/main\n# 7be3f10 HEAD@{1}: commit: Tune ingress timeouts\ngit branch rescue 7be3f10      # give the lost commit a name again\ngit switch rescue\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">git reflog -15\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># 41ac9d2 HEAD@{0}: reset: moving to origin/main\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># 7be3f10 HEAD@{1}: commit: Tune ingress timeouts\u003c/span>\ngit branch rescue 7be3f10      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># give the lost commit a name again\u003c/span>\ngit switch rescue\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Any commit you can name by hash is safe once a branch points to it. This works for commits, not for uncommitted edits, which Git never saw. A deleted branch is also recoverable: find its last hash in the reflog and recreate it with \u003ccode>git branch NAME HASH\u003c/code>. For commits that are in no reflog at all, \u003ccode>git fsck --lost-found\u003c/code> lists unreachable objects.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"find-the-guilty-commit-with-bisect\">Find the guilty commit with bisect\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>You know version 1.3 worked and the current one does not, with 200 commits between. \u003ccode>git bisect\u003c/code> does a binary search and needs only about eight steps:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git bisect start\ngit bisect bad                 # current commit is broken\ngit bisect good v1.3.0         # this one worked\n# Git checks out a commit in the middle. Test it, then say good or bad.\ngit bisect good\ngit bisect bad\n# ... until it prints: 3f9c2ab is the first bad commit\ngit bisect reset\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">git bisect start\ngit bisect bad                 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># current commit is broken\u003c/span>\ngit bisect good v1.3.0         \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># this one worked\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># Git checks out a commit in the middle. Test it, then say good or bad.\u003c/span>\ngit bisect good\ngit bisect bad\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># ... until it prints: 3f9c2ab is the first bad commit\u003c/span>\ngit bisect reset\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Better still, automate it: \u003ccode>git bisect run ./test.sh\u003c/code>, where the script exits 0 for good and non-zero for bad. A small reproducible test is the most valuable thing to write during any regression hunt.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"who-changed-this-and-why\">Who changed this, and why\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git blame -L 40,60 deploy.sh        # who last changed each line\ngit log -S&quot;max_retries&quot; --oneline   # commits that added or removed this text\ngit log -G&quot;retry.*=&quot; -p -- deploy.sh\ngit log --follow -- scripts/backup.sh   # history across renames\ngit show 3f9c2ab                    # the full change and message\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">git blame -L 40,60 deploy.sh        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># who last changed each line\u003c/span>\ngit \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> -S\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;max_retries&quot;\u003c/span> --oneline   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># commits that added or removed this text\u003c/span>\ngit \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> -G\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;retry.*=&quot;\u003c/span> -p -- deploy.sh\ngit \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> --follow -- scripts/backup.sh   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># history across renames\u003c/span>\ngit show 3f9c2ab                    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># the full change and message\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Blame shows the last commit to touch a line, which may only be a reformat. Use \u003ccode>git blame -w -C\u003c/code> to ignore whitespace and detect moved code, and follow the commit message and linked ticket to learn the reason. The aim is to understand why the code is the way it is before you change it, not to blame a person.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"confusing-states\">Confusing states\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Detached HEAD\u003c/strong>: you checked out a commit or tag, not a branch. Commits made here belong to no branch. If you want to keep them, run \u003ccode>git switch -c new-branch\u003c/code> right away. \u003cstrong>Committed on the wrong branch\u003c/strong>: create a branch at the current commit with \u003ccode>git branch feature\u003c/code>, then move the wrong branch back with \u003ccode>git reset --hard origin/main\u003c/code> (after making sure the new branch exists). \u003cstrong>Unfinished operations\u003c/strong>: \u003ccode>git status\u003c/code> tells you when you are in the middle of a merge, rebase or cherry-pick and which command continues or aborts it. \u003cstrong>Untracked clutter\u003c/strong>: \u003ccode>git clean -n\u003c/code> previews what \u003ccode>git clean -fd\u003c/code> would delete. Read \u003ccode>git status\u003c/code> first; Git usually tells you the next safe command.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You ran a hard reset and lost two commits. What is the first command to try, and what will it show?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> git reflog. It lists where HEAD pointed before, including the hashes of the lost commits that you can branch from.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Committed work can nearly always be recovered.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-which-release-broke-the-report\">Incident: Which release broke the report?\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The monthly billing report began producing wrong totals, and nobody knew which of 214 commits since the last good release was responsible.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"git bisect start v2.8.0 v2.7.0\ngit bisect run ./check_totals.sh\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git bisect start v2.8.0 v2.7.0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Bisecting: 107 revisions left to test after this (roughly 7 steps)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git bisect run ./check_totals.sh\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">running ./check_totals.sh\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">...\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">3d51f8a is the first bad commit\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    Round currency in the aggregate query\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A small script that compared the report with known totals was enough for git bisect run to find the commit in seven steps: a change that rounded values too early. The fix was a one-line change and the script became a regression test in the pipeline. Without bisect, the team would have read hundreds of diffs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Panicking and re-cloning instead of checking the reflog\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Committing in detached HEAD and losing the work\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using blame to assign fault instead of to learn the reason\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running git clean -fd without the -n preview\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"recover-with-the-reflog","text":"Recover with the reflog"},{"id":"find-the-guilty-commit-with-bisect","text":"Find the guilty commit with bisect"},{"id":"who-changed-this-and-why","text":"Who changed this, and why"},{"id":"confusing-states","text":"Confusing states"},{"id":"incident-which-release-broke-the-report","text":"Incident: Which release broke the report?"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Pro Git: Git Internals, Maintenance and Data Recovery","u":"https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Internals-Maintenance-and-Data-Recovery"},{"t":"git-bisect documentation","u":"https://git-scm.com/docs/git-bisect"}],"text":"At some point Git will show you something frightening: \"detached HEAD\", a branch that vanished, a commit you cannot find, a build that broke between two releases. In nearly every case the data is still there. Git almost never deletes committed work immediately, and it also keeps a private journal of where your branches pointed. Knowing how to read that journal and how to search history turns panic into a routine investigation. What you will learn Find and recover lost commits with git reflog Locate the commit that introduced a bug with git bisect Answer who changed this and why with blame and log -S Understand detached HEAD, wrong-branch commits and unfinished operations Recover with the reflog The reflog records every time HEAD or a branch moved on your machine: commits, resets, rebases, checkouts. It is local and expires after weeks, but it is the safety net for almost every \"I lost my work\" moment. git reflog -15 # 41ac9d2 HEAD@{0}: reset: moving to origin/main # 7be3f10 HEAD@{1}: commit: Tune ingress timeouts git branch rescue 7be3f10 # give the lost commit a name again git switch rescue Any commit you can name by hash is safe once a branch points to it. This works for commits, not for uncommitted edits, which Git never saw. A deleted branch is also recoverable: find its last hash in the reflog and recreate it with git branch NAME HASH . For commits that are in no reflog at all, git fsck --lost-found lists unreachable objects. Find the guilty commit with bisect You know version 1.3 worked and the current one does not, with 200 commits between. git bisect does a binary search and needs only about eight steps: git bisect start git bisect bad # current commit is broken git bisect good v1.3.0 # this one worked # Git checks out a commit in the middle. Test it, then say good or bad. git bisect good git bisect bad # ... until it prints: 3f9c2ab is the first bad commit git bisect reset Better still, automate it: git bisect run ./test.sh , where the script exits 0 for good and non-zero for bad. A small reproducible test is the most valuable thing to write during any regression hunt. Who changed this, and why git blame -L 40,60 deploy.sh # who last changed each line git log -S \"max_retries\" --oneline # commits that added or removed this text git log -G \"retry.*=\" -p -- deploy.sh git log --follow -- scripts/backup.sh # history across renames git show 3f9c2ab # the full change and message Blame shows the last commit to touch a line, which may only be a reformat. Use git blame -w -C to ignore whitespace and detect moved code, and follow the commit message and linked ticket to learn the reason. The aim is to understand why the code is the way it is before you change it, not to blame a person. Confusing states Detached HEAD : you checked out a commit or tag, not a branch. Commits made here belong to no branch. If you want to keep them, run git switch -c new-branch right away. Committed on the wrong branch : create a branch at the current commit with git branch feature , then move the wrong branch back with git reset --hard origin/main (after making sure the new branch exists). Unfinished operations : git status tells you when you are in the middle of a merge, rebase or cherry-pick and which command continues or aborts it. Untracked clutter : git clean -n previews what git clean -fd would delete. Read git status first; Git usually tells you the next safe command. Predict first. You ran a hard reset and lost two commits. What is the first command to try, and what will it show? Your guess Answer: git reflog. It lists where HEAD pointed before, including the hashes of the lost commits that you can branch from. Committed work can nearly always be recovered. Incident: Which release broke the report? Incident The monthly billing report began producing wrong totals, and nobody knew which of 214 commits since the last good release was responsible. $ git bisect start v2.8.0 v2.7.0 Bisecting: 107 revisions left to test after this (roughly 7 steps) "}});